1
00:00:00,099 --> 00:00:04,160
If you get caught in the middle of a big,
open field during a lightning storm, which

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:07,279
of the following uniforms would be most likely
to keep you safe?

3
00:00:07,279 --> 00:00:12,780
A thick wetsuit; a superman costume; a medieval
coat of armor; or a birthday suit?

4
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:16,880
If you answered "medieval coat of armor,"
you might be a little crazy--but you'd also

5
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:17,880
be right.

6
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:21,780
You'd be crazy, of course, because lightning
is much more likely to strike metal than to

7
00:00:21,780 --> 00:00:23,490
strike rubber, fabric, or bare skin.

8
00:00:23,490 --> 00:00:27,700
The reason is that lightning bolts are just
long streams of fast-flowing electrons looking

9
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:32,940
for the easiest path from point A to point
B, and no everyday material provides an easier

10
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:34,660
path than metal.

11
00:00:34,660 --> 00:00:38,579
So why would a material that lures lightning
bolts keep you safe during a thunderstorm?

12
00:00:38,579 --> 00:00:42,909
Ironically, for the exact same reason it attracts
the the lightning in the first place: metal

13
00:00:42,909 --> 00:00:45,589
is a great conductor of electricity.

14
00:00:45,589 --> 00:00:49,839
Electrons glide so easily over metals that
they barely penetrate into the surface.

15
00:00:49,839 --> 00:00:53,481
And if an electrical current happens to be
moving over a hollow metal container, like

16
00:00:53,481 --> 00:00:58,129
a can or a box or even a welded coat of armor,
the current won't reach the inside of the

17
00:00:58,129 --> 00:00:59,129
container.

18
00:00:59,129 --> 00:01:03,699
Physicists call this kind of container a Faraday
cage . Or, in the case of the steel-woven

19
00:01:03,699 --> 00:01:07,990
clothing worn by linemen working on high-voltage
wires, a Faraday suit.

20
00:01:07,990 --> 00:01:12,840
\hIn fact, your car is an everyday example
of a Faraday cage, which is why--despite what

21
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:16,689
you may have heard about rubber tires--it's
actually the closed metal body surrounding

22
00:01:16,689 --> 00:01:18,350
you that keeps you safe.

23
00:01:18,350 --> 00:01:21,610
It channels lightning around, rather than
through you.

24
00:01:21,610 --> 00:01:25,719
Of course, if you're away from your car and
get caught in an open field during a storm,

25
00:01:25,719 --> 00:01:30,240
chances are slim that you'll have a medieval
coat of armor or high voltage line-suit handy.

26
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:35,070
In that case, whether you're naked or in costume,
your body unfortunately happens to be a better

27
00:01:35,070 --> 00:01:39,810
electrical conductor than both air and soil,
so it provides a great shortcut for traveling

28
00:01:39,810 --> 00:01:40,810
current.

29
00:01:40,810 --> 00:01:43,670
Stand upright, and you're the fastest route
for a descending lightning bolt.

30
00:01:43,670 --> 00:01:48,080
Lie down, and you're the best path for current
racing along the ground from a nearby strike.

31
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:52,049
So the best thing to do is crouch low and
keep your feet close together.

32
00:01:52,049 --> 00:01:53,640
Crouching low is obvious.

33
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:56,970
But similarly important, when your feet are
right next to each other, your legs don't

34
00:01:56,970 --> 00:02:01,130
make for much of a shortcut for the current
to get from A to B. And even if they are the

35
00:02:01,130 --> 00:02:04,450
best path for the lightning, when your feet
are the only thing touching the ground, then

36
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:08,870
the current will most likely travel up one
leg and down the other, missing critical organs

37
00:02:08,870 --> 00:02:11,780
like your heart--something these cows couldn't
avoid.

38
00:02:11,780 --> 00:02:15,563
But, actually, the real best thing to do is
avoid lightning altogether--armor or no armor--and

39
00:02:15,563 --> 00:02:18,140
head indoors when you spot a storm on the
horizon.

